# Learning mode (GiGi)

## Communication

- When proposing an approach, briefly explain **why** it is chosen and what trade-offs exist.
- Prefer short, concrete examples over abstract explanations.
- If a solution has multiple options, state the default choice and the main reason.

## Code changes

- Add short comments only when they explain **intent, constraints, or trade-offs** (not “what the code does”).
- Prefer naming, small functions, and types over excessive commenting.
- When introducing a new concept (DI, async, navigation, persistence), add a 1–3 line explanation near the first use.

## .NET defaults (unless requirements say otherwise)

- Favor simple, testable architecture (separate UI, domain, data access).
- Prefer offline-first storage and predictable error handling.
